WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:03.550
ADAM EVANS: Good
morning, everybody.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.050
Thank you for coming.

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:06.170
My name is Adam Evans.

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:10.980
I work in the Google Maps
API part of Google, part

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.870
of the big Google Cloud team.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.140
This is the first of eight
sessions in the Maps track

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.840
here at Google Cloud Next.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.780
We're going to focus
today's session

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.810
on talking about
optimizing and making

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.870
more efficient your
business operations using

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.486
Google's location APIs.

00:00:30.486 --> 00:00:33.060
I did want to point out we have
quite a few other sessions as

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:33.559
well.

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:36.029
So if you have
questions about applying

00:00:36.029 --> 00:00:38.070
Maps in your application
that don't get addressed

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:42.510
by this session today, it's a
big rich, deep suite of APIs.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.690
Please be sure to attend
some of the other sessions.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:46.980
Come and connect with some
of the folks in the Maps team

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:47.771
after this session.

00:00:47.771 --> 00:00:49.860
We'd love to make sure
everybody goes home

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.480
with their questions answered.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:52.950
We'll have some Q&amp;A as well.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:55.980
So today, my
colleague David and I

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.850
are going to explain why
Google believes that location

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.550
is really critical to
business operations,

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.140
talk a little bit
about trends that we

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.540
see in the use of
maps and location

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:11.430
in more B2B nontraditional
use cases for Maps,

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:16.362
beyond just looking
at visualizing a map.

00:01:16.362 --> 00:01:17.820
Just to get a sense
of the audience

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.944
here to make sure we can
make this relevant to you,

00:01:19.944 --> 00:01:22.080
can I get a show of
hands for those who

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.102
identify more as a developer?

00:01:25.102 --> 00:01:27.310
And how about those who
identify more on the business

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.070
side of their business?

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.786
How about both?

00:01:30.786 --> 00:01:32.230
Everybody is a
hybrid these days.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:32.620
There we go.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:33.119
OK.

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:33.790
That's great.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:35.170
And how many of
those in the room

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.840
are currently implementing
location or Maps

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.580
tools of any kind in
their applications today?

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:41.080
All right.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.670
So there's some
deep fluency here.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.940
How many have not, but have
some interest in using location

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.229
in their tools?

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:48.520
All right, again, that's great.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.380
So is a pretty
broad cross-section,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.420
which I think gives some--

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:56.200
gives us an obligation to get
a little bit of broad context,

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.240
as we get started here.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.482
The world is a really
busy place, as we know.

00:02:01.482 --> 00:02:03.190
At Google Maps we see
a lot of businesses

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:05.110
asking us questions
every day about how

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:08.900
to make better sense
of their employees,

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.936
their customers,
their suppliers,

00:02:10.936 --> 00:02:13.060
the operations of their
business as it moves around

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.140
in the world.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.990
And this is an area where
Google's deep understanding

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:19.000
of the world and
the places in it

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.570
can be useful to you
and to your businesses.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:24.670
Picture a major
city that perhaps

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.750
is trying to do urban planning,
plan traffic management, plan

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:32.560
the movement of people
and goods, zoning,

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.421
sprawl, expansion.

00:02:34.421 --> 00:02:36.670
They may have invested
hundreds of millions of dollars

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.970
in capital in perhaps
expensive sensors

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:43.090
to monitor the movement
of vehicles over roads.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:45.670
This is the kind of place where
Google's real-time traffic

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:49.930
information, sourced from
billions of connected devices

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:52.960
and connected Google Maps
phones with location opted in,

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:56.200
can help drive better business
decisions for that city.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.160
We want to be able to answer
questions such as where should

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.149
we plan the next expressway?

00:03:02.149 --> 00:03:03.940
We also want to help
individual businesses,

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.565
from the really large
corporations down

00:03:05.565 --> 00:03:07.570
to the very small startup,
to answer questions

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:11.020
like where should my
salesperson plan a visit based

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:12.342
on density of their customers?

00:03:12.342 --> 00:03:14.050
What is the most
efficient route for them

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:17.500
to visit 10 or 15 customers in
a very short visit, a layover,

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.460
for instance?

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.640
If I'm a taxi company,
how many vehicles

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.500
should I dispatch
just after midnight

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.699
on New Year's Eve, based on
historical traffic patterns?

00:03:26.699 --> 00:03:28.990
These are questions that
Google can and wants to answer

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.152
through our location APIs.

00:03:31.152 --> 00:03:32.860
In other words, we
want to make it easier

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.260
to bring the location
of the real world,

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.450
this high resolution,
deeply accurate,

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.420
scalable map that Google
stands up for our consumers.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.790
We want to make that
available through APIs

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:44.226
so that you can
access those data

00:03:44.226 --> 00:03:45.850
and make decisions
about your business.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.220
Location is something
we just naturally use

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:54.330
to connect us, with our
friends, with our colleagues,

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.215
with each other,
with our customers.

00:03:56.215 --> 00:03:58.590
If you think about this, think
about what's on your phone

00:03:58.590 --> 00:03:59.010
right now.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.260
Think about the number of
apps, hopefully in addition

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.650
to Google Maps, the mobile
app, that you use that already

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.100
natively use location.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.320
A ride share app
like Via, who offers

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.120
$5 flat-rate rides
in New York City,

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.860
is powered by Google Maps.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:15.900
How can they do
that at that cost?

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.089
Because the infrastructure
costs of building

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:20.640
on Google's platforms,
including our APIs,

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.950
are so low and so
efficient that they

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.860
can plan an entire ride
sharing application

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.320
around an easy-to-build
mobile app

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.330
and support from Google
and our partners,

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.610
to put together a ride
sharing application.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.639
Think about a vacation rental
application like Tripping,

00:04:35.639 --> 00:04:37.680
where they can provide
deep context about a place

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.344
you might want to visit,
context such as how far are

00:04:42.344 --> 00:04:44.010
these hotels from the
places of interest

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:45.600
that I'd like to visit?

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.690
Google has a point of interest
database with hundreds

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:52.472
of millions of points
with the type of places

00:04:52.472 --> 00:04:53.930
that might interest
you on a visit.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:56.040
Do you want to visit
an amusement park?

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.846
Is there a church nearby?

00:04:57.846 --> 00:04:59.970
Is there a place that I
can stop so my daughter can

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:02.430
go to the bathroom on the trip?

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.890
And speaking of
which, Google Maps

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:06.540
also helps to answer that--

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.090
I have a four-year-old girl
and I've had to answer that

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:09.590
question--

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:13.029
Daddy, how long until we get to
Space Mountain at Disney World?

00:05:13.029 --> 00:05:14.820
So we can provide that
immersive experience

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.620
for location and maps.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:21.380
If you've ordered a coffee
recently through your Starbucks

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:23.330
mobile app, you
want to make sure

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.356
that it's hot when you arrive.

00:05:25.356 --> 00:05:27.230
A lot of people order
the coffee when they're

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:28.229
standing in a long line.

00:05:28.229 --> 00:05:31.612
You can even order it in
the car on the way in.

00:05:31.612 --> 00:05:33.070
How do we know that
coffee is going

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:34.240
to be hot when you arrive?

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.550
We need to know exactly
when that person is pulling

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:38.258
into the parking lot
so it can be sitting

00:05:38.258 --> 00:05:40.316
there steaming on the counter.

00:05:40.316 --> 00:05:41.940
Traffic can be pretty
bad where I live,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:45.960
so if I order the coffee
in the car, on my app,

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:47.850
Starbucks can tell how
long it's going to be,

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:50.475
using Google's real-time traffic
and the predictive travel time

00:05:50.475 --> 00:05:53.730
features, such that they
only spin up the order

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:57.630
so that the barista has
prepared my cappuccino just

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:00.230
in time that's still
ready on the counter.

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.850
The feature I really
need is, hey Adam,

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.567
you're one coffee short
of a wakeful morning.

00:06:06.567 --> 00:06:07.650
You've overshot the place.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:09.687
Here's a little warning,
and perhaps a geofence

00:06:09.687 --> 00:06:12.270
can tell me to turn around and
go back and pick up the coffee.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.630
So I like to ask
this question a lot.

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:19.200
So far I've talked about
consumer experiences

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.750
and the experiences that we
have as consumers using location

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:22.290
in apps.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.151
But what does this
mean for a business?

00:06:24.151 --> 00:06:25.650
I think from the
show of hands a lot

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:28.770
of people here work in
and operate businesses

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:32.700
that don't necessarily have a
citizen or a consumer interface

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:35.250
as their primary means of
connecting with the world.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:37.320
Maybe you want to
connect with a supplier.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:40.170
Maybe you want to connect
with your own employees.

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.570
What does this trend
toward using location

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:43.830
to connect with people--

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:46.460
what does it mean
for your business?

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:48.020
Anybody have any guesses?

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.894
You can shout it out if
you know of what this

00:06:49.894 --> 00:06:53.410
is, what we're showing here.

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:56.124
These are the
movements of phones.

00:06:56.124 --> 00:06:57.790
These are the movements
of mobile phones

00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:02.310
with location opted in
tracked for Google Maps.

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:04.500
And you can see the
movements through time

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:05.310
throughout the day.

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:06.870
The living, breathing
city, as people

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:10.440
commute generally from
the suburbs into downtown.

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:15.420
This is possible to drive all
those really complex decisions

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:18.570
about planning that I talked
about, whether it's for a city,

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:21.720
or for a taxi company, or
for a dispatch company.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.520
All of these
decisions are driven

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:24.810
by a massive amount of data.

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:29.430
What's relevant here is that to
get the position of something

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:32.070
to within a few meters--

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:34.800
this is a major, major change
that's kind of happened quietly

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:36.220
around us in the last decade.

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:40.500
15 years ago, the cost to
get the position of something

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:44.310
within a few meters was two
or three orders of magnitude

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:45.835
higher than it is right now.

00:07:45.835 --> 00:07:47.877
You used to need this
differential GPS equipment.

00:07:47.877 --> 00:07:49.376
If you don't know
what that is, it's

00:07:49.376 --> 00:07:51.120
like an antenna
and backpack system

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.060
that surveyors would
use to understand

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:57.110
where a point was on the
ground pretty accurately.

00:07:57.110 --> 00:08:00.900
That's now subsidized by your
mobile carrier and the location

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:03.450
driven by every mobile phone.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.860
And that's really done a
lot to connecting people

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:08.399
with the places they need to be.

00:08:08.399 --> 00:08:09.940
But what we think
is really relevant,

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.010
and there's been a lot of talk
about the Internet of Things

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:13.840
and the impact that
that might have

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.390
on your business and
the world in general,

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:19.870
is that if devices become
connected, billions of devices,

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:23.740
not just phones that
natively include geolocation,

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:26.680
but perhaps a little
tag for my dog

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:28.660
who has a bad habit
of running away,

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:31.990
perhaps a fitness
band that can track

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:33.549
how far you've been running.

00:08:33.549 --> 00:08:36.340
Maybe every single piece
of manufacturing equipment,

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:38.620
such as expensive
construction equipment used

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:42.977
on a construction site
that are prone to theft,

00:08:42.977 --> 00:08:44.560
the tracking of
everything is becoming

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.010
cheap, an order of
magnitude or two cheaper.

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:50.580
And as we know, just in
general for trends that happen,

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:53.170
is that when something
gets a lot cheaper,

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:56.260
new business models become
possible and old ones get

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:56.830
under stress.

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:59.119
So the reason that we want
to talk about location

00:08:59.119 --> 00:09:00.910
and what it means for
your business today--

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:02.368
we're going to show
some examples--

00:09:02.368 --> 00:09:03.670
is that we think it's critical.

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:05.440
Our customers are
coming to us saying,

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.230
I need to have a strategy
to incorporate location

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:10.120
in my business operations
because everything

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.820
is becoming connected and
everything has a position.

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:19.240
Increasingly, that position is
not necessarily shown on a map.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:24.120
We believe at Google that we're
entering the fourth phase,

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.610
or the fourth wave, of mapping.

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:31.990
Now, paper maps are
something with hundreds,

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:33.940
and even thousands
of years of history.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:36.440
Cartography has been
around for a long time,

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:42.004
since the first maps created by
the ancient Greeks and Ptolemy,

00:09:42.004 --> 00:09:43.420
all the way through
the paper maps

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:46.374
that your parents probably
printed out, those AAA TripTiks

00:09:46.374 --> 00:09:48.040
that you'd have, that
big stack of paper

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:50.870
you'd have to take a
trip with your family.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:54.910
Perhaps you started
using digital maps

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:57.190
through geographic
information systems,

00:09:57.190 --> 00:09:59.650
if you work with them in
business or as a citizen you

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:02.530
might have started using them
with the launch of Yahoo maps

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:07.090
or MapQuest or when Google Maps
launched, 12 years ago now.

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:08.920
And then we moved
into a more dynamic,

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:10.760
real-time picture of the world.

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:13.115
The third wave, and that's
where most of us are today.

00:10:13.115 --> 00:10:14.740
We can get a real-time
picture of where

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:19.750
we are, such as those apps that
I showed earlier on screen.

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:21.660
Automotive GPS
dashboards are now

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:23.785
integrated to give you a
real-time picture of where

00:10:23.785 --> 00:10:25.660
you and all the
vehicles are around you,

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:27.490
in order to give you traffic.

00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:29.140
But we think we're
entering into a wave

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:30.790
where location can
be more intelligent.

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:32.142
And the actual map--

00:10:32.142 --> 00:10:34.600
which, let's be honest, we
don't need to always glance down

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:37.624
at our phone to understand
the estimated time before I

00:10:37.624 --> 00:10:38.665
arrive at my destination.

00:10:38.665 --> 00:10:40.840
All I need is the ETA.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:44.140
Maybe I need it just
as a voice prompt.

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:47.221
Maybe my delivery
driver just needs

00:10:47.221 --> 00:10:49.720
to know exactly how far behind
schedule or ahead of schedule

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:51.250
they are.

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:54.040
The directions are being given
to them through the dashboard

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:55.060
equipment.

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:56.890
So we think we're
moving to a world

00:10:56.890 --> 00:10:59.530
where predictive analytics
and machine learning

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:04.150
are allowing devices, people,
and vehicles to connect

00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:06.550
without the need to visualize
that on a map all the time.

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:09.070
And then ultimately,
if billions of devices

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:12.820
become connected, that
we'll be able to extract

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:15.960
some patterns of information and
learn more about our business.

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:17.960
And this is where we think
it's really exciting.

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:19.786
So the reason I've
given you this preamble

00:11:19.786 --> 00:11:21.410
is to get to an
interesting story here.

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:26.250
I had the privilege of
talking to the chief operating

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:28.500
officer of a really
interesting software

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.210
company, small company.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:34.740
And they started trying to
fill a niche in dispatch tools

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:36.510
for small businesses.

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:38.640
Now, big companies who
operate huge fleets

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:41.130
have long had access
to dispatch software.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:43.900
That would mean telling an
ambulance or a truck driver

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:46.500
in a large fleet where
to be at what time

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.939
and when to pick up a
load and drop it off.

00:11:48.939 --> 00:11:50.730
They'd have tools in
the vehicle and they'd

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:53.280
have tools for the dispatcher
back at the head office.

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:56.730
But small companies, like
a florist, perhaps a small,

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:59.010
mom-and-pop-shop
delivery company,

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:00.630
you might have two
or three trucks,

00:12:00.630 --> 00:12:03.630
a couple of taxis to drop off
the flowers before the wedding.

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:04.880
They never had access to this.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:06.900
So the software company
I was speaking with

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:07.920
built a beautiful tool.

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:11.200
It was a tablet that you
can buy and the software

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:13.290
is a service model, every month.

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:15.240
It came with a
telematics device which

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:17.580
allowed, through the
airwaves, to send

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:20.950
a little bit of information
back to that company

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:23.670
and describe where
the taxi was, describe

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:27.139
where that florist was, and have
the same kind of robust tools.

00:12:27.139 --> 00:12:28.680
When we went to
speak with them, they

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:31.771
were using a competitor system
to Google Maps at the time.

00:12:31.771 --> 00:12:34.020
And we said would you like
to transfer over to Google?

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:35.640
They said yes, but
you know, to us

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:38.850
visualizing that data on a
map, yeah, that's interesting.

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:40.710
Google Maps are great
and they provide me

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:42.950
with satellite pictures
that we really like

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:44.440
and some place information.

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:46.920
But Adam, what we really
want to talk to you about

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:50.010
is something that will make
a difference in our business.

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:51.990
Because the challenge
that this person had is

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:53.160
they had 10,000 customers.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.410
You think about the costs
of servicing, any of you who

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:57.270
own a business or
operate the business,

00:12:57.270 --> 00:12:59.460
you know how much it costs
to keep a customer happy,

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:00.720
informed, and serviced.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.540
They had 10,000 customers and
they were a small company.

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:05.610
Their customers only
had half a dozen

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:07.110
or maybe 10 vehicles each.

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:10.710
That's 100,000 vehicles,
10,000 customers,

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:12.900
think about what that
means for their margins.

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:15.370
To grow, they had to
service one more--

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:18.120
10,001 customers, 10,002.

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:20.040
They wanted to
grow and break out.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:23.070
And they said, Adam,
can Google help me?

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:25.860
We worked with the Google
Cloud platform analytics team

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:27.636
to get creative.

00:13:27.636 --> 00:13:29.010
They said that
they had the right

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:32.820
to look at the movements
of all of those 100,000

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:37.050
vehicles in their
downstream customers' fleet.

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:39.840
Every two minutes, they would
ping one of those vehicles

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:41.310
and get a little
data packet back.

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:42.610
It was about 100 kilobytes.

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:45.660
And in there was the
speed of the vehicle,

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:49.140
what it was carrying--
flowers, for example--

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:53.280
how fast was it going, was it
parked, where was it parked,

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:55.200
the latitude and
longitude of that vehicle.

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:56.880
And all of that
position information

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:58.200
came back to a server.

00:13:58.200 --> 00:13:59.700
And you think about
what that means.

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:02.010
100,000 vehicles, roughly
every two minutes,

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:06.454
that's 72 million data points a
day across the aggregate fleet.

00:14:06.454 --> 00:14:08.620
Now they weren't allowed
to identify their customers

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:11.800
by name, but they took that
aggregated information,

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:13.990
and through some
data science, they

00:14:13.990 --> 00:14:17.794
were able to draw statistically
significant patterns

00:14:17.794 --> 00:14:19.210
of the movement
of their customers

00:14:19.210 --> 00:14:20.600
to learn some great things.

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:22.270
Here's one of the
things they learned.

00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:26.500
They learned that a
significant number of customers

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.260
idled longer in front
of a coffee shop

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:32.040
than they did in
front of a warehouse.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:34.210
That's interesting.

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:38.790
They learned that
two-axle truck traffic

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:40.950
in and out of the
port of Vancouver

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:43.487
was greater than
four-axle truck traffic.

00:14:43.487 --> 00:14:45.570
Now I don't know what to
do with that information.

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:47.520
But there are organizations
who really want

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:48.645
to know that kind of thing.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:50.520
So think about this.

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:53.490
A small software company,
who had a tough margin growth

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:56.640
business for small mom
and pop delivery shops,

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:00.870
continued to run that business
and then analyzed their data

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:02.719
using Google platform
and location.

00:15:02.719 --> 00:15:05.010
Keep in mind, the location
is critical for all of this.

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:08.130
To get the address, to get the
place type from Google's Places

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:11.910
API, to get all of the
contextual information required

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:15.060
to run that data science,
you need the location.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:18.540
So they ran location analytics
on the aggregated database

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:20.250
and launched a new business.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:22.150
They are now a data
science company,

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:25.860
and they sell packages of
information to a hedge fund,

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:27.300
to an insurance company.

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:29.354
And they've learned a
lot about data science

00:15:29.354 --> 00:15:31.395
by working with Google
and working with location.

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:34.630
And the reason I love
that story is twofold.

00:15:34.630 --> 00:15:37.980
One, small companies get
access to incredible technology

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:41.190
in a way that only big companies
used to have access to before.

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:44.610
But two, they did all of that
in a short period of time

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:47.130
and with a relatively
low cost base.

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:49.680
They are leveraging
the location data

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:52.770
from their own existing
business to learn new things.

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:54.310
So we think this is exciting.

00:15:54.310 --> 00:15:56.730
So lets talk a little
bit about some use cases

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:58.380
here beyond that fleet company.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:03.454
How tracking, in particular,
drives business decisions.

00:16:03.454 --> 00:16:05.620
We have a lot of companies
that build asset tracking

00:16:05.620 --> 00:16:08.800
solutions for more than
just tracking trucks.

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:12.640
They might track the
position of their employees,

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:14.260
the efficiency of
their sales force

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:17.770
as they go to visit different
customers in the world.

00:16:17.770 --> 00:16:21.310
They might be
looking at optimizing

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:23.140
the number of vehicles
that they allocate

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:25.690
to a problem, such as the taxis
on New Year's Eve scenario

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:26.710
that I gave.

00:16:26.710 --> 00:16:28.210
So lets look at a
few of these here.

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:33.209
In order to provide a great
customer service experience--

00:16:33.209 --> 00:16:34.750
how many people in
the room here have

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:37.840
had that frustrating moment
where your cable company needs

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:39.910
to come and service
your cable, and says

00:16:39.910 --> 00:16:42.190
please be home between
the hours of 8:00 a.m.

00:16:42.190 --> 00:16:43.600
and next week?

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:45.770
Right, we've all been there.

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:48.280
So part of the challenge
there that that company really

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:49.510
runs up against-- we
know because we've

00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:50.968
worked with some
of these companies

00:16:50.968 --> 00:16:53.680
to improve that customer service
experience-- the challenge they

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:55.750
have is a logistical one.

00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:57.430
They have one service
person who has

00:16:57.430 --> 00:16:59.290
many service calls to attend.

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:01.790
And then they have the
customer, that's you.

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:05.050
You may be at home
hoping to have somebody

00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:07.960
come to visit you, but they have
a delay from the previous job.

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:12.250
Using location to connect the
customers with the service

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:14.907
people is a simple thing that
can improve customer service

00:17:14.907 --> 00:17:16.990
and make a massive difference
to your market share

00:17:16.990 --> 00:17:19.119
if you're in a service
business like installing

00:17:19.119 --> 00:17:23.980
cable, but perhaps even
for delivering the mail.

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:26.079
Customers can get
a real-time status

00:17:26.079 --> 00:17:27.760
for the position
of their package,

00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:29.980
if it's being tracked
in a vehicle, and even

00:17:29.980 --> 00:17:32.620
a notification of the estimated
time before it's there.

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:35.290
So that you can be at work
and say, oh, delivery person

00:17:35.290 --> 00:17:36.910
is coming in the
next 20 minutes,

00:17:36.910 --> 00:17:39.610
I'm just going to pop home
quickly, pick up the package,

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:40.210
sign for it.

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:46.790
Companies like Fleetminder have
been using our asset tracking

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:50.870
platform, using our maps,
using our real-time traffic,

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:53.339
and our places
information, to make

00:17:53.339 --> 00:17:55.880
their business more efficient
for their downstream customers.

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:58.580
And we have a great quote here
from the CIO of Fleetminder

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:00.920
that says Google's mapping
solution has helped them

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:04.940
cut their own customers'
inefficient driving in half

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:08.340
and reduce incorrect
deliveries by 20%.

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:10.140
That is the kind of
statement that you want

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:11.414
to make to your customers--

00:18:11.414 --> 00:18:13.830
To say that we've reduced your
inefficiency significantly,

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:15.500
your fuel costs, and your time.

00:18:18.010 --> 00:18:19.960
Another really interesting
use case for this

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:22.960
is the full utilization of
all the equipment out there.

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:24.627
If you've ever seen
a construction site,

00:18:24.627 --> 00:18:26.626
you might notice that
there's a lot of equipment

00:18:26.626 --> 00:18:28.570
there, anything from
the great big digger

00:18:28.570 --> 00:18:30.790
all the way down to
a generator running

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:33.100
some remote lights or
a scissor jack that

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:36.130
allows people to work up on the
second floor in a construction

00:18:36.130 --> 00:18:37.210
project.

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:39.130
This equipment is
typically rented

00:18:39.130 --> 00:18:42.710
through a series of businesses
that maintain inventory.

00:18:42.710 --> 00:18:44.981
Equipment gets beaten up
and needs service regularly.

00:18:44.981 --> 00:18:47.230
And it needs to be maintained
in different inventories

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:48.820
around the world.

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:51.040
If you are running a
large construction project

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:52.739
and look to rent
this equipment, you

00:18:52.739 --> 00:18:54.280
need to be sure that
the inventory is

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:55.470
there and available.

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:57.970
And it's been a major challenge
for businesses in this space

00:18:57.970 --> 00:18:59.820
to maintain availability.

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:03.190
You can lose business if you
don't have equipment that's

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:05.140
ready and has been serviced.

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:07.600
This was a challenge faced
by one of our customers

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:10.960
who implemented tracking
devices using geolocation

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:12.650
on all of their equipment.

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:14.320
They were able to reduce theft.

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:16.714
They were able to monitor
the service intervals

00:19:16.714 --> 00:19:18.130
and the number of
hours of service

00:19:18.130 --> 00:19:20.260
that each piece of
equipment had gone through,

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:22.030
so that they could
be sure that when

00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:25.620
the equipment was being
allocated to a new job,

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:27.070
that it was ready for service.

00:19:27.070 --> 00:19:29.770
But the location
component was critical.

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:33.400
If you went to rent
equipment in Dayton, Ohio,

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:37.060
and it wasn't there, their
database system in the back end

00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:43.170
needed to check the real-time
location of other availability.

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:45.660
So over in Cincinnati,
there may have

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:48.930
been the same kind of equipment
available in another branch.

00:19:48.930 --> 00:19:51.630
That led to a massive increase
in customer satisfaction

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:53.770
for the business that
implemented full scheduling

00:19:53.770 --> 00:19:55.680
and utilization by just
tracking the position

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:58.680
of their equipment.

00:19:58.680 --> 00:20:01.160
By having a real-time
view of vehicles,

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:05.000
dispatchers can track
global orders and shipments

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:07.790
and have a dashboard
picture of the operations

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:08.540
of their business.

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:10.220
Now, many businesses
are doing this today,

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:11.886
and where Google's
really been enhancing

00:20:11.886 --> 00:20:15.050
it has been providing
contextual information.

00:20:15.050 --> 00:20:16.346
Is someone in front--

00:20:16.346 --> 00:20:16.970
where are they?

00:20:16.970 --> 00:20:18.860
I have their latitude
and longitude.

00:20:18.860 --> 00:20:19.990
What's the address?

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:23.210
And using Google's geocoder,
I can get the address.

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:25.460
And using Google's
Places API, can now

00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:27.290
see what type of addresses.

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:29.150
Are they in front
of that warehouse,

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:32.930
or in front of the coffee shop
idling a little bit too long?

00:20:32.930 --> 00:20:34.490
I can get live
weather and traffic

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:37.369
by correlating that
position to other feeds,

00:20:37.369 --> 00:20:39.410
and really get an accurate
picture of my business

00:20:39.410 --> 00:20:42.350
as it is now, including
meeting dynamic customer

00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:44.000
needs such as rerouting orders.

00:20:46.070 --> 00:20:47.820
I want to tell another
interesting story,

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:53.250
working with a company that
builds small device trackers

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:55.974
for very sensitive goods.

00:20:55.974 --> 00:20:57.390
Can you imagine
if you were trying

00:20:57.390 --> 00:21:00.720
to ship a drug sample, a
package of drug samples,

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:04.590
or if you were shipping an
organ in the organ donor system?

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:06.600
These are high value goods.

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:07.479
They're insured.

00:21:07.479 --> 00:21:09.270
They're insured by an
insurance company who

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:11.880
writes a policy based on
the likelihood of that good

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:12.924
to have to be replaced.

00:21:12.924 --> 00:21:15.090
And when you're insuring
something like drug samples

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:17.100
or a box of Rolex
watches, you want

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:19.902
to make sure you understand
the risk to that shipment.

00:21:19.902 --> 00:21:22.110
This company that we work
with makes a tracker, about

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:24.720
this big, and it has some
interesting things in it.

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:27.180
Its tracker has a
humidity sensor,

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:28.740
which I guess is
important if you're

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:31.884
trying to understand the health
of some drug samples, humidity

00:21:31.884 --> 00:21:32.550
and temperature.

00:21:32.550 --> 00:21:34.770
It has a tip-tilt
sensor that knows

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:36.360
whether the box was dropped.

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:39.090
It has accelerometers to know
whether it fell off a shelf

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:40.500
and might be damaged.

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:43.410
But the key thing was that
before they came and worked

00:21:43.410 --> 00:21:46.440
with Google, the
package would arrive

00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:48.870
you would open it up and
then find out the damage.

00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:50.880
You would remove the
tracker, plug it in,

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:53.790
and understand what happened.

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:55.920
But it was all after
the event, and it

00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:58.540
had a huge impact on
the customers' rate

00:21:58.540 --> 00:22:00.540
that they paid for the
insurance of the package,

00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:02.790
because they could not trace
where the event happened.

00:22:02.790 --> 00:22:04.590
The liability was not known.

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:09.370
So the rates to insure untracked
goods were really high.

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:11.889
This company implemented,
through really low cost--

00:22:11.889 --> 00:22:13.930
remember I was talking
about that trend, the cost

00:22:13.930 --> 00:22:16.007
to find the position of
something is now cheap.

00:22:16.007 --> 00:22:18.340
Used to be really expensive
to know where something was,

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:20.320
now they put a small Wi-Fi--

00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:23.000
low battery power
requirements, and really,

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:25.490
really low cost to put a Wi-Fi
connector in their device.

00:22:25.490 --> 00:22:28.960
And any time that package
moved through the world,

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:30.700
it might occasionally
see a Wi-Fi signal.

00:22:30.700 --> 00:22:32.825
On your phone right now,
how many Wi-Fi hotspots do

00:22:32.825 --> 00:22:34.480
you think you can see, roughly?

00:22:34.480 --> 00:22:35.320
10?

00:22:35.320 --> 00:22:36.670
20?

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:38.740
As these packages move
throughout the world,

00:22:38.740 --> 00:22:40.090
they would see a Wi-Fi hotspot.

00:22:40.090 --> 00:22:42.160
Google offers a
global geolocation

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:46.240
API that has a georeferenced
position for Wi-Fi hotspots.

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:50.350
And that means that these
devices' positions are known

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:51.820
as they move through the world.

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:54.220
So when the box was
dropped, it can now

00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:55.660
be correlated to a position.

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:58.720
If the box was tipped, we
know exactly where that was.

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:01.030
Geofences and real-time
alerts could be sent up.

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:03.400
And this had a material
impact on the cost

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:05.080
of the insured
goods for everything

00:23:05.080 --> 00:23:06.230
that this company provides.

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:07.896
They have a huge
advantage in the market

00:23:07.896 --> 00:23:10.270
now, over their competitors,
because they implemented

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:11.500
location in their tracking.

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:16.590
Vehicle telematics are an
interesting and broad business.

00:23:16.590 --> 00:23:19.680
We could spend an entire
session just on this alone.

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:21.480
Companies such as
Mojio and Dash have

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:24.000
built platforms,
connected to Google,

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:26.790
that allow a better
relationship between the driver

00:23:26.790 --> 00:23:29.710
and the vehicle, and all
sorts of other businesses.

00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:31.260
Including an
insurance company, who

00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:33.960
might want to offer you a
discount on your driving

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:36.900
if you're a safe driver and you
don't accelerate hard, or brake

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:39.720
hard, or run a stoplight.

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:42.360
It might have a great connection
with the auto dealership.

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:44.010
If you go to buy
your car, you notice

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:45.960
how they want you to
come back for service.

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:47.460
I don't know about
you, but I'm busy

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:49.320
and I often miss my
service intervals,

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:51.750
and you get the occasional
phone call from the dealership.

00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:53.910
Your car is due
for an oil change.

00:23:53.910 --> 00:23:58.200
With an easy connection
between the car's position,

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:00.300
tracking the number of
miles that it's driven,

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:03.960
providing regular metadata
through a location connected

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:07.170
device, you now get
an alert on your phone

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:09.300
when the car's due
for an oil change.

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:11.250
Before you come in for
service, the dealership

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:13.230
has a lot more information
about your vehicle,

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:15.540
about how it's been driven,
If it's been driven hard,

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:17.676
if it's ready for
its service or not.

00:24:17.676 --> 00:24:19.050
This allows a
better relationship

00:24:19.050 --> 00:24:20.880
between the company
and the driver.

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:25.642
So I've just given a whole
