00:26:03.790 --> 00:26:05.960
Now, I'll go into a
little bit more detail

00:26:05.960 --> 00:26:07.920
of what these APIs do.

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:11.460
But first, I want to
show you some stats.

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:17.680
Google loves statistics,
or maybe that's just me.

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:21.270
Two million websites and apps
are using the Google Maps API

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:22.260
every week.

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:27.510
This makes the Google Maps APIs
one of the most popular APIs

00:26:27.510 --> 00:26:29.490
on the internet.

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:32.370
Google has maps of
over 200 countries

00:26:32.370 --> 00:26:36.360
worldwide, which represents
about 40 million miles

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:38.280
of mapped roads.

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:41.190
And we're providing
transit directions daily

00:26:41.190 --> 00:26:44.474
on the order of 1
billion kilometers a day

00:26:44.474 --> 00:26:45.390
of transit directions.

00:26:45.390 --> 00:26:49.860
And that's drawing from
Google's real-time traffic data

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:54.150
that we now have in
over 100 countries.

00:26:54.150 --> 00:26:57.600
So we have a set of
APIs that give access

00:26:57.600 --> 00:27:00.030
to this information to you
for your own applications.

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:02.080
And we have APIs
for every platform.

00:27:02.080 --> 00:27:06.810
So whether you're building a web
application, iOS application,

00:27:06.810 --> 00:27:11.460
Android application, or a
back-end server application,

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:14.010
we have APIs that to allow
you to bring this data

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:15.869
into your own application.

00:27:15.869 --> 00:27:17.160
So what are some of these APIs?

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:18.868
I'm not going to go
through all of these.

00:27:18.868 --> 00:27:20.370
You see, we have
