00:27:20.370 --> 00:27:21.900
But I'll go through
some of the highlights

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:23.670
and then I'll walk you through
some architectures on how

00:27:23.670 --> 00:27:26.231
you can incorporate them into
your own cloud applications

00:27:26.231 --> 00:27:26.730
as well.

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:30.600
But we have the Maps APIs to
start with, so our JavaScript

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:32.820
API or Street View APIs.

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:35.490
These allow you to bring
Maps visualizations

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:37.270
into your application.

00:27:37.270 --> 00:27:39.530
But we also have
the Places APIs,

00:27:39.530 --> 00:27:43.330
which gives you access to
Google's database of over 100

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:45.070
million points of
interest globally.

00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:47.890
And that's business
information, business reviews,

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:49.510
business photos.

00:27:49.510 --> 00:27:51.610
We also have an
auto-complete service,

00:27:51.610 --> 00:27:55.060
which gives you that familiar
Google type-ahead experience

00:27:55.060 --> 00:27:57.710
for address entry.

00:27:57.710 --> 00:28:00.800
We have our routing algorithm
and our Directions API

00:28:00.800 --> 00:28:03.320
and Distance Matrix
API which draw

00:28:03.320 --> 00:28:07.040
upon that real-time
traffic information.

00:28:07.040 --> 00:28:10.040
And I'll walk you through a
few of these in more detail.

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:12.980
So what do these
APIs allow you to do?

00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:15.710
So, first of all, we
talked about visualization.

00:28:15.710 --> 00:28:17.660
Nearly all information
in the world today

00:28:17.660 --> 00:28:20.580
has some sort of geospatial
context to it, right?

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:24.350
So the ability to display
this information on a map

00:28:24.350 --> 00:28:26.630
allows you to make sense
of this information in ways

00:28:26.630 --> 00:28:30.730
that viewing a tabular set of
data doesn't allow you to do.

00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:32.230
Second is location services.

00:28:32.230 --> 00:28:33.880
What do I mean by
location services?

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:36.760
Well, not everything
needs a map display,

00:28:36.760 --> 00:28:39.680
and not every thing has a
display to begin with, right?

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:42.430
So if you're building an
IoT device, for example,

00:28:42.430 --> 00:28:44.800
it just needs to know
where it is in the world

00:28:44.800 --> 00:28:47.170
and make use of
that information,

00:28:47.170 --> 00:28:49.210
make use of that context.

00:28:49.210 --> 00:28:51.990
So we give you the
APIs that allow

00:28:51.990 --> 00:28:53.410
you to just pull
those data feeds

00:28:53.410 --> 00:28:57.020
without the need of a display.

00:28:57.020 --> 00:29:00.310
Our APIs give you access to
our movement optimization

00:29:00.310 --> 00:29:03.400
algorithms, so our
Driving Directions.

00:29:03.400 --> 00:29:05.410
We have a Distance
Matrix API, which

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:09.310
allows you to calculate
actual driving

00:29:09.310 --> 00:29:11.620
distance and actual
driving times

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:15.490
using current and
historical traffic data.

00:29:15.490 --> 00:29:18.400
And this all comes
together to give you

00:29:18.400 --> 00:29:19.710
true location intelligence.

00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:21.660
So I think where
the real power comes

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:24.330
in is mixing your own
data, your own customer

00:29:24.330 --> 00:29:27.990
data, your own business
data, with Google's data,

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:32.760
rich location data, to actually
form actionable insights that

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:34.854
allow you to make better
business decisions.

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:40.120
So how does this come
together with Cloud?

00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:45.910
So we all know that GCP provides
an incredible range of products

00:29:45.910 --> 00:29:49.690
and infrastructure for
storage, and for processing,

00:29:49.690 --> 00:29:51.375
and machine learning too.

00:29:51.375 --> 00:29:54.400
I think where Google
Maps adds value,

00:29:54.400 --> 00:29:57.870
and the difference here is our
set of APIs provide content.

00:29:57.870 --> 00:30:00.610
It provides content
and data enrichment.

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:02.560
So when you fuse
these two together,

00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:05.320
you have a back-end
architecture,

00:30:05.320 --> 00:30:07.162
you have Google
content, merged together

00:30:07.162 --> 00:30:08.620
in one place with
your own content.

00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:13.980
I'm going to walk you through
a few block diagrams which

00:30:13.980 --> 00:30:16.380
demonstrate how these
APIs can be used

00:30:16.380 --> 00:30:18.044
in a real application
with the cloud,

00:30:18.044 --> 00:30:20.460
and walk through, a little
more in detail, some of the use

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:22.440
cases that Adam described,
how you'd actually

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:25.440
go about building
something like this.

00:30:25.440 --> 00:30:28.850
So imagine a fleet
tracking application

00:30:28.850 --> 00:30:31.100
or connected vehicle
type application.

00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:35.000
So you have a vehicle
generating a tremendous amount

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:37.999
of sensor data,
tons of telemetry,

00:30:37.999 --> 00:30:39.540
you need to stream
that to the cloud.

00:30:39.540 --> 00:30:42.020
So obviously GCP provides
a set of products

00:30:42.020 --> 00:30:43.040
to ingest that data.

00:30:43.040 --> 00:30:44.714
Things like Pub/Sub
and Dataflow,

00:30:44.714 --> 00:30:46.130
that allow you to
ingest that data

00:30:46.130 --> 00:30:50.300
and store that data in the
cloud, and query that data.

00:30:50.300 --> 00:30:54.080
But if you want to find some
actual insights from that data,

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:57.170
you might need to merge it
with Google's own location data

00:30:57.170 --> 00:30:59.047
to add some more value to it.

00:30:59.047 --> 00:31:00.380
So what does it allow you to do?

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:02.171
So we have a set of
APIs that will give you

00:31:02.171 --> 00:31:03.890
road network understanding.

00:31:03.890 --> 00:31:08.090
For example, our speed limit API
to access our speed limit data,

00:31:08.090 --> 00:31:10.490
globally, which
would perhaps allow

00:31:10.490 --> 00:31:14.540
you to audit your fleet of
drivers for dangerous driving

00:31:14.540 --> 00:31:16.040
behavior.

00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:18.440
We have something called
a snap-to-road API

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:21.470
so you're getting GPS
positions off these trucks,

00:31:21.470 --> 00:31:22.940
you know they're
not going to be--

00:31:22.940 --> 00:31:25.034
there's a lot of noise
in the GPS measurements.

00:31:25.034 --> 00:31:26.450
So our snap-to-road
API allows you

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:28.430
to snap those to the
nearest road network,

00:31:28.430 --> 00:31:30.440
and basically use
Google's road network

00:31:30.440 --> 00:31:32.840
to clean up those measurements
that you're taking.

00:31:32.840 --> 00:31:35.210
We talked about
the Directions API.

00:31:35.210 --> 00:31:37.430
We have an Elevation
API, a global database

00:31:37.430 --> 00:31:40.850
of elevation data, that
allow you to figure out

00:31:40.850 --> 00:31:45.050
the incline, for example, that
a vehicle might be going on.

00:31:45.050 --> 00:31:46.650
And we mentioned the Places API.

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:49.767
You know what Adam
mentioned is, if one

00:31:49.767 --> 00:31:52.100
of your drivers and your fleet
is spending a lot of time

00:31:52.100 --> 00:31:53.660
in a particular location,
you might want to know,

00:31:53.660 --> 00:31:54.743
