We have a lot of
documentation online

00:35:19.510 --> 00:35:21.730
that really allows you
to kind of self-serve

00:35:21.730 --> 00:35:23.440
and get into this in depth.

00:35:23.440 --> 00:35:26.590
There are Google people around
today and throughout the rest

00:35:26.590 --> 00:35:28.780
of the sessions who
can help with the how

00:35:28.780 --> 00:35:30.400
to implement some of this.

00:35:30.400 --> 00:35:32.500
But just to put bookends
on this session,

00:35:32.500 --> 00:35:34.210
I started off
talking about not so

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:36.220
much how to implement
a location strategy,

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:40.720
but why we think that big
businesses and small businesses

00:35:40.720 --> 00:35:44.410
and citizens and governments
should consider a location

00:35:44.410 --> 00:35:46.030
strategy to make
better decisions.

00:35:47.380 --> 00:35:51.370
And to tell the story, I'm
going to relate another customer

00:35:51.370 --> 00:35:52.244
engagement we had.

00:35:52.244 --> 00:35:53.660
Customer engagement
sounds cheesy.

00:35:53.660 --> 00:35:57.010
We got invited to work
with a company that

00:35:57.010 --> 00:36:00.670
is almost the opposite
of that small fleet

00:36:00.670 --> 00:36:01.810
scenario I gave you.

00:36:01.810 --> 00:36:04.240
I was talking a bit about
the company with two or three

00:36:04.240 --> 00:36:07.630
florists, or maybe half
a dozen small taxis

00:36:07.630 --> 00:36:09.070
or something like that.

00:36:09.070 --> 00:36:09.970
This is the inverse.

00:36:09.970 --> 00:36:13.210
We were invited to meet
with the CTO of one

00:36:13.210 --> 00:36:15.850
of the largest trucking
companies in the world.

00:36:15.850 --> 00:36:17.980
And they had an
interesting problem,

00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:19.750
and it wasn't one that
you would think off

00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:22.300
the top was a technology
problem related

00:36:22.300 --> 00:36:24.460
to maps and digital tools.

00:36:24.460 --> 00:36:26.800
We got invited to tour
their warehouse where they

00:36:26.800 --> 00:36:29.470
service these huge big rigs.

00:36:29.470 --> 00:36:31.630
We're talking to big
transport trailers

00:36:31.630 --> 00:36:33.670
with very large
engines and large loads

00:36:33.670 --> 00:36:38.170
that they would pull over long
haul routes around the world.

00:36:38.170 --> 00:36:39.640
With so many trucks
in their fleet,

00:36:39.640 --> 00:36:43.640
they had to have very efficient
operations and servicing

00:36:43.640 --> 00:36:44.140
systems.

00:36:44.140 --> 00:36:45.681
So you can imagine
just how efficient

00:36:45.681 --> 00:36:47.845
it was for them to
maintain these trucks.

00:36:48.880 --> 00:36:52.450
And he pointed in the corner
at a pallet, a shipping pallet

00:36:52.450 --> 00:36:53.860
full of boxes.

00:36:53.860 --> 00:36:56.917
And in the boxes were lots
and lots of alternators.

00:36:56.917 --> 00:36:58.750
And if you don't know
what an alternator is,

00:36:58.750 --> 00:37:00.310
it's like a generator
that recharges

00:37:00.310 --> 00:37:01.944
the battery in an engine.

00:37:01.944 --> 00:37:03.610
And in a truck,
apparently, these things

00:37:03.610 --> 00:37:05.320
are very expensive.

00:37:05.320 --> 00:37:07.314
And they were running
into a challenge.

00:37:07.314 --> 00:37:08.980
They tried a lot of
different suppliers,

00:37:08.980 --> 00:37:10.480
but they were failing.

00:37:10.480 --> 00:37:11.980
And they were
failing prematurely,

00:37:11.980 --> 00:37:13.899
and they did not know why.

00:37:13.899 --> 00:37:15.190
It was costing them some money.

00:37:15.190 --> 00:37:16.606
It wasn't breaking
their business,

00:37:16.606 --> 00:37:18.310
but it was costing
them some money.

00:37:18.310 --> 00:37:21.880
They were having to preemptively
replace the alternators

00:37:21.880 --> 00:37:23.650
in every single one
of their trucks,

00:37:23.650 --> 00:37:25.557
as they came in for service.

00:37:25.557 --> 00:37:26.890
And this was costing them money.

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:28.240
They were buying
more alternators

00:37:28.240 --> 00:37:29.656
than they knew
they really needed,

00:37:29.656 --> 00:37:33.010
but they did not know when
they were going to fail.

00:37:33.010 --> 00:37:36.280
They implemented a Google
Maps-based asset tracking

00:37:36.280 --> 00:37:39.460
platform for everything
from their dispatch side

00:37:39.460 --> 00:37:42.262
to getting local information
about where a truck was.

00:37:42.262 --> 00:37:43.720
But one of the
byproducts of it was

00:37:43.720 --> 00:37:47.050
that they were able to get
that ping of information

00:37:47.050 --> 00:37:49.480
from the vehicle
every minute or so.

00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:51.280
And across all the
trucks, they had

00:37:51.280 --> 00:37:54.940
hundreds of millions of points
per day across their fleet.

00:37:54.940 --> 00:37:57.970
So they would know at any
moment, every minute or so,

00:37:57.970 --> 00:37:59.980
a lot of information
about that vehicle.

00:37:59.980 --> 00:38:02.440
So from there they might
get the mass airflow sensor,

00:38:02.440 --> 00:38:04.360
which tells you how
hard the driver was

00:38:04.360 --> 00:38:05.830
pressing on the throttle.

00:38:05.830 --> 00:38:07.630
They might get the fuel level.

00:38:07.630 --> 00:38:11.860
They might get the health of
certain sensors in the engine.

00:38:11.860 --> 00:38:13.540
And one of the
things they measured

00:38:13.540 --> 00:38:16.180
was how much power was
going across that part that

00:38:16.180 --> 00:38:18.790
was breaking, how much
electricity voltage was

00:38:18.790 --> 00:38:21.100
going through that alternator.

00:38:21.100 --> 00:38:24.224
And they started to correlate
it, in a big data effort,

00:38:24.224 --> 00:38:26.140
essentially, run on the
Google Cloud platform,

00:38:26.140 --> 00:38:28.900
based on the location
data of every single one

00:38:28.900 --> 00:38:30.250
of these trucks.

00:38:30.250 --> 00:38:32.510
And they started
to see a pattern.

00:38:32.510 --> 00:38:34.280
They saw that there
was a voltage drop

00:38:34.280 --> 00:38:36.229
in the alternators that failed.

00:38:36.229 --> 00:38:38.270
And so they said, oh, I
can smell something here,

00:38:38.270 --> 00:38:39.770
there is a smoking gun.

00:38:39.770 --> 00:38:41.270
There was a voltage
drop in the ones

00:38:41.270 --> 00:38:42.860
that were failing prematurely.

00:38:42.860 --> 00:38:45.800
But how do we know which
ones are going to do this?

00:38:45.800 --> 00:38:47.480
How do we know when
that voltage drop--

00:38:47.480 --> 00:38:48.500
you know, what's
correlated with?

00:38:48.500 --> 00:38:49.970
How can we change
driver behavior?

00:38:49.970 --> 00:38:51.740
What can we do?

00:38:51.740 --> 00:38:55.340
They used the Google Elevation
API, a little-known API

00:38:55.340 --> 00:38:59.150
that Google serves that has a
global picture of the mean sea

00:38:59.150 --> 00:39:01.850
level, at any position or
latitude and longitude,

00:39:01.850 --> 00:39:03.710
you know, the height
above mean sea level.

00:39:03.710 --> 00:39:05.420
So you can actually figure
out whether someone's

00:39:05.420 --> 00:39:08.000
driving, over a long route,
whether they're generally going

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:09.530
up a grade or down a grade.

00:39:09.530 --> 00:39:13.220
And it turns out the vehicles
that spent more than 20%

00:39:13.220 --> 00:39:15.460
of their route driving
up a steep grade

00:39:15.460 --> 00:39:18.510
were the ones that
saw that voltage drop.

00:39:18.510 --> 00:39:20.749
They were able to identify
by doing analytics

00:39:20.749 --> 00:39:22.290
on the location of
their entire fleet

00:39:22.290 --> 00:39:24.000
and looking at the
sensor information,

00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:27.240
looking at the location,
correlating it with context

00:39:27.240 --> 00:39:29.520
from Google's
Elevation API, they

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:32.820
were able to preemptively
repair and replace

00:39:32.820 --> 00:39:35.910
those alternators that were the
ones that are going to fail.

00:39:35.910 --> 00:39:39.800
And they cut down an entire
pallet load every month

00:39:39.800 --> 00:39:41.730
of these alternators
that they needed to buy.

00:39:41.730 --> 00:39:44.010
Now, the reason I'm
telling you this story

00:39:44.010 --> 00:39:48.220
is not because the headline
is "Trucking Company Saves

00:39:48.220 --> 00:39:50.520
a Couple Hundred Thousand
Dollars in Alternators."

00:39:50.520 --> 00:39:52.170
That's not the headline.

00:39:52.170 --> 00:39:56.460
The headline is an alternator
parts supply salesperson

00:39:56.460 --> 00:40:00.595
hangs up her phone and says,
you know, I just lost an order.

00:40:00.595 --> 00:40:01.970
The trucking
company doesn't want

00:40:01.970 --> 00:40:04.250
to buy alternators this
month, or not as many anyway,

00:40:04.250 --> 00:40:05.990
and I can't figure out why.

00:40:05.990 --> 00:40:09.650
The reason this is important
is that if our premise is right

00:40:09.650 --> 00:40:14.030
and that the cost of location is
opening up new business models,

00:40:14.030 --> 00:40:17.240
there is an efficiency coming
to virtually every business.

00:40:17.240 --> 00:40:20.030
Not just ride share
apps, not just vacation

00:40:20.030 --> 00:40:23.000
rental apps, not just
your coffee ordering app,

00:40:23.000 --> 00:40:24.740
but to every brick
and mortar business

00:40:24.740 --> 00:40:26.660
and traditional old
businesses, location

00:40:26.660 --> 00:40:29.930
is starting to force efficiency.

00:40:29.930 --> 00:40:32.090
This change will force
all sorts of businesses

00:40:32.090 --> 00:40:34.252
to have to include
location in their strategy.

00:40:34.252 --> 00:40:35.960
So the question we'd
like to leave people

00:40:35.960 --> 00:40:39.140
with after telling stories like
this, and providing some tools,

00:40:39.140 --> 00:40:41.435
as David has done in
the how-to, is maybe

00:40:41.435 --> 00:40:44.060
when you get back to the office
and you're talking a little bit

00:40:44.060 --> 00:40:48.020
about what you learned at Google
Next, ask some of the people

00:40:48.020 --> 00:40:50.930
that you work with
how they see location,

00:40:50.930 --> 00:40:52.600
and how it works in your world.

00:40:52.600 --> 00:40:56.094
You know your business and your
industry and the reality of it

00:40:56.094 --> 00:40:57.510
far better than
Google ever would.

00:40:57.510 --> 00:40:59.930
But how can location
help you craft a strategy

00:40:59.930 --> 00:41:02.510
to get out in front of some
of these big changes that

00:41:02.510 --> 00:41:06.230
are coming thanks to optimizing
our business using location?

00:41:06.230 --> 00:41:08.510
Thanks, everybody,
for sticking around.

00:41:11.210 --> 00:41:13.060
Thank you.

